---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Answer from '../components/Answer.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Longer hose, same fittings?', a: 'Yes, fittings are independent of length. Pressure drop is not. Very long 1/4″ ID hoses on a small electric feel weak at the tip.' },
  { q: 'Can I keep the old gun?', a: 'Yes if the new hose matches the gun inlet. That is the whole point of measuring both ends.' },
];
---
<Base title="Replacement pressure washer hose" description="Buy a replacement hose by fitting, stem size, ID, and PSI — not by the machine’s color or a “universal M22” title." path="/replacement-hose/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Replacement hose</p>
    <h1>Buy the hose last</h1>
    <Answer>
      Write four numbers: end A fitting, end B fitting, hose ID, PSI. Then shop. “Fits Ryobi / Simpson / Craftsman” is an ad, not a measurement.
    </Answer>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><a href="/identify/">Identify</a> pump outlet and gun inlet.</li>
      <li>Decide whether to stay threaded or <a href="/m22-to-quick-connect/">convert to QC</a>.</li>
      <li>Pick ID: 1/4″ for many electrics; 5/16″ or 3/8″ for gas that actually makes flow.</li>
      <li>PSI rating ≥ machine. Burst rating is not working pressure.</li>
      <li>Read <a href="/hose-ends/">both ends</a> of the listing.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If the machine is 15 mm and you want a cheap 14 mm hose, the 15↔14 coupler is a legitimate part of the bill of materials — cheaper than a unicorn 15 mm × 15 mm rubber hose.</p>
    <Faq items={faqs} />
  </div>
</Base>
